  (all tabular matter is SVG figures), so the table-scroll-container concern does not arise.
- A4 print: zero clipped elements on all 7 pages; all 8 figures fit (right edge 778 < 794);
  the Answers and marking guidance section renders in print (answerHeadings present on
  unit-assessment).
- Bare-URL link text (advisory, tool-reported): Further reading links in topic-01..04 use
  the raw URL as link text (e.g. "https://open.umn.edu/..."). Recorded as an advisory
  accessibility finding in the report; not a hard defect.

## Tool exit code and the two flagged defects (investigated and overruled)

`render-inspect.mjs` exited 1 with two DEFECT lines:

    fig-U2-3.light: text "larger, cheaper per unit" past viewBox right edge
    fig-U2-3.dark:  text "larger, cheaper per unit" past viewBox right edge

Investigation (evidence: renders-.../fig-U2-3-svg.png, fig-U2-3-right-label.png, and the
measurements below): the flagged label is one of the pyramid's two ROTATED arrow captions
(transform="rotate(90 704 239)", text-anchor=middle). The tool's geometry check uses
getBBox(), which for these transformed <text> elements returns the PRE-ROTATION local box
(x=620.8..787.7 for the right label) and compares it against the 780x470 viewBox - hence
the flag. Measuring the element as actually rendered (getBoundingClientRect, chromium
149.0.7827.0):

    "faster, costlier per unit"  screenRect x=64..80,   y=158.5..319   (left label)
    "larger, cheaper per unit"   screenRect x=700..716, y=155.8..322.7 (right label)

Both rendered labels lie fully inside the 780x470 viewBox; nothing is clipped. Visual
crops at 3x device scale confirm the complete, unclipped label text (see
fig-U2-3-right-label.png; the final glyph of "unit" is whole, with clearance to the edge).
fig-U2-3.dark.svg is geometry-identical to fig-U2-3.svg (byte-diff with colour values
normalised: no difference), so the same conclusion covers the dark variant. The remaining
14 variant checks (fig-U2-1..U2-2, U2-4..U2-8, light and dark) are clean, including
wordmark-overlap checks.

Conclusion: the two flags are a measurement artifact of getBBox() on rotated text, not a
rendered defect. The inspection as a whole found no confirmed visual or print defect:
every page loads, every figure is served with meaningful alt text, nothing overflows or is
clipped at 1280px, 360px or A4 print, and the answers section renders in print.

render-review exit_code: 0 (inspection complete; the tool's exit 1 is overruled on the
recorded evidence above).
